### Terraform Version

```shell
Terraform v1.6.4
on linux_amd64
+ provider registry.terraform.io/carlpett/sops v0.7.2
+ provider registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/google v5.5.0
+ provider registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/google-beta v5.5.0
+ provider registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/random v3.5.1
```


### Use Cases

When you have to dynamically apply a resource with for_each depending of another resource which doesn't exists, for example to create a service account and apply a set of permissions to it located in a "local", the terraform says that the key cannot be detemined and doesn't work. I understand that this is part of the check to avoid conflicts for example, but using some "tricks" can be avoided and then the key can be determined even when the resource doesn't exists yet. The problem is that terraform doesn't try to determine the key when the value is unknown, and then such tricks doesn't work.

### Attempted Solutions

For example, if I have a new service account and I have to apply two roles to it based in a local variable, I have to dynamically generate the map for the for_each:

```
resource "google_service_account" "service_account" {
  account_id = "nice-service-account"
  display_name = "Nice Service Account"
}

locals {
  permissions = [
    {
      service_account = google_service_account.service_account.email
      roles = [
        "role1",
        "role2"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

resource "google_project_iam_member" "accounts_roles" {
  for_each = { for a in chunklist(flatten([for i in local.permissions : setproduct([i["service_account"], i["roles"])]), 2) : "${a[0]}_${a[1]}" => {
    account = a[0],
    role    = a[1]
  } }
  role     = each.value.role
  member   = each.value.account
  project  = local.settings.project.id
}
```

In the above example the terraform will fail saying that the keys cannot be determined until apply. I understand this behaviour because terraform will try to determine conflicts in names and similar before to apply. Which is not able to do the terraform is to avoid it even then the keys can be determined but depends of data that is undetermined yet.

### Proposal


For example, if I convert the above example to this one:

```
resource "google_service_account" "service_account" {
  account_id = "nice-service-account"
  display_name = "Nice Service Account"
}

locals {
  permissions = [
    {
      service_account = google_service_account.service_account.email
      roles = [
        "role1",
        "role2"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

resource "google_project_iam_member" "accounts_roles" {
  for_each = {
    for idx, value in flatten([
        for i in local.permissions : [
            for acc, r in setproduct(i["service_account"], i["roles"]) : {
                account = acc,
                role    = r
            }
        ]
    ]) : idx => value
  }
  role     = each.value.role
  member   = each.value.account
  project  = local.settings.project.id
}
```

The values remains unknown, but the keys can be easily determined because are derivated from the number of entries and not their values. In this case the emails of the service account is unknown, but you know that there are two entries:

```
[
    {
        account = *unknown*
        role = "role1"
    },
    {
        account = *unknown*
        role = "role2"
    },
]
```
So you are able to determine that the idx values are 0 and 1 and thus the resources keys are not unknown anymore.

Will be nice to be able to apply resorces when they are unknown and the keys are predictable like in the above example, because applying it manually is not a problem because you use a target to create the first resource and problem solved, but in a CI/CD is harder to do it.

DISCLAIMER: I made this examples on the fly without testing it and maybe are not correct. This is because my case of use is slightly different, but is based in the same root problem.

In my case the service account is created outside a module, and inside the module I determine the resources to which apply a permission with a for loops. The resources and the service account can not exists, but the way to determine the keys is the same (1 account 2 resources = 1 x 2 = 2 = {0 = x, 1 = x}, 1 account 3 resources = 1 x 3 = 3 = {0 = x, 1 = x, 2 = x}...).

Sorry if I have not explained it clear enough, but english is not my main language and this adds and extra difficulty to me.

