### Use Cases

I work with some resource that require attributes specified in different ways depending on if the resource is being newly created or already exists and is being updated.  For example consider this:

This example resource requires setting `source_details` block when creating new but updates require setting `related_ids` block for updates.
```hcl
resource "my_example" "testing" {
  description = "My example"
  source_details {
    type = "id"
    ids = [
      "id1",
      "id2"
    ]
  }
}
```

then, if a later update is required you must use this syntax:
```hcl
resource "my_example" "testing" {
  description = "My example"
  related_ids {
    ids = [
      "id1",
      "id2"
    ]
  }
}
```


### Attempted Solutions

My current work-around is to have a variable `first_build` (bool, default false) defined then I write my module like this:

```hcl
resource "my_example" "testing" {
  description = "My example"
  dynamic "related_ids" {
    for_each = var.first_build ? [] : var.ids
    content {
      ids = related_ids.value
    }
  }

  dynamic "source_details" {
    for_each = var.first_build ? var.ids : []
    content {
      type = "id"
        ids = source_details.value
      }
    }
  }
}
```


### Proposal

I would enjoy `when` blocks like this example whereby the evaluation of each conditional block is assembled along with unconditional code to a final resource for processing. For example:

```hcl
resource "my_example" "testing" {
  description = "My example"
  when {
    action = "updating"
    content {
      "related_ids" {
        ids = var.ids
      }
    }
  }

  when {
    action = "creating"
    content {
      "source_details" {
        type = "id"
        ids = var.ids
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Here `description` is always considered then either the first "updating" block or second "creating" block is included based on the current action underway. There could be more attributes/blocks set before the when blocks or after and each when could contain any number of attributes or blocks/nested blocks (with or without dynamic clauses).

